WEBVTT

special-04-1
00:00:00.937 --> 00:00:03.860
“Women are naturally good at caring. What
a blessing.”

special-04-2
00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.581
“Care is noble work. Women's
relationality is a source of strength.”

special-04-3
00:00:07.581 --> 00:00:09.707
Two statements. Opposite judgments.

special-04-4
00:00:09.707 --> 00:00:12.897
The same premise.

special-04-5
00:00:15.023 --> 00:00:17.946
Judgments do not change the pay slip.

special-04-6
00:00:19.275 --> 00:00:23.527
“Better suited to homemaking” is produced
by repeatedly assigning that role.

special-04-7
00:00:23.527 --> 00:00:27.780
But within feminism, one path also led
elsewhere.

special-04-8
00:00:28.843 --> 00:00:34.002
In the mid-to-late 1970s, some US radical
feminists turned toward “cultural

special-04-9
00:00:34.002 --> 00:00:34.690
feminism”:

special-04-10
00:00:34.690 --> 00:00:38.555
rather than dismantling the gendered
division of labor, they affirmed feminine

special-04-11
00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:40.537
traits: care, connection and motherhood.

special-04-12
00:00:41.069 --> 00:00:42.663
Here is the problem.

special-04-13
00:00:42.663 --> 00:00:47.979
If relationality is women's essence, it
shares a premise with “women should look

special-04-14
00:00:47.979 --> 00:00:49.042
after the home.”

special-04-15
00:00:49.042 --> 00:00:51.168
Tradition says: therefore, it is your
duty.

special-04-16
00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:53.825
This says: therefore, it is your glory.

special-04-17
00:00:55.951 --> 00:00:59.263
The more motherhood, housework and
emotional labor are praised, the more

special-04-18
00:00:59.263 --> 00:01:00.735
they resemble an unpaid calling.

special-04-19
00:01:00.735 --> 00:01:02.861
Praise does not change who takes leave.

special-04-20
00:01:02.861 --> 00:01:06.619
This is not every radical feminist's
position. Millett, Firestone and others

special-04-21
00:01:06.619 --> 00:01:09.240
sought to dismantle precisely this
division of labor.

special-04-22
00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:12.429
Then leave relationships aside. Talk
about the individual.

special-04-23
00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:17.532
Mainstream liberal thought promises an
“individual”: self-owning, freely

special-04-24
00:01:17.532 --> 00:01:19.871
choosing, personally responsible.

special-04-25
00:01:19.871 --> 00:01:22.528
But when this person has a fever, who
takes leave?

special-04-26
00:01:22.528 --> 00:01:25.530
Who remembers his follow-up appointment?
Who picks up the children when he works

special-04-27
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:25.718
late?

special-04-28
00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:29.408
Carole Pateman argues that the social
contract's “individual” assumes someone

special-04-29
00:01:29.408 --> 00:01:31.565
at home carries the burden of his
dependency.

special-04-30
00:01:31.565 --> 00:01:35.817
His independence is built on someone
else's care.

special-04-31
00:01:36.348 --> 00:01:39.538
Women are caught between two paths.

special-04-32
00:01:39.538 --> 00:01:43.790
Want equality? Become that “individual”
and offload care.

special-04-33
00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:47.511
Onto whom? Often poorer women. More in
Episode 6.

special-04-34
00:01:47.511 --> 00:01:51.586
Want care recognized? Remain in
relationships, in the place tradition

special-04-35
00:01:51.586 --> 00:01:52.826
already assigned you.

special-04-36
00:01:52.826 --> 00:01:57.841
Pateman calls this the “Wollstonecraft
dilemma.”

special-04-37
00:01:57.841 --> 00:02:02.195
Both paths end alike: someone carries
another's dependency and is not counted

special-04-38
00:02:02.195 --> 00:02:03.156
as “independent.”

special-04-39
00:02:04.219 --> 00:02:07.408
A psychoanalytic reading, not an
empirical finding.

special-04-40
00:02:07.408 --> 00:02:11.660
For Lacan, no one truly possesses the
“phallus.”

special-04-41
00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:15.324
The masculine position is the fantasy of
having it: being whole, lacking nothing,

special-04-42
00:02:15.324 --> 00:02:15.912
owing no one.

special-04-43
00:02:15.912 --> 00:02:20.696
The liberal individual resembles an ego
pretending it has not been castrated.

special-04-44
00:02:21.759 --> 00:02:23.885
He does depend on others.

special-04-45
00:02:23.885 --> 00:02:26.542
He simply refuses to acknowledge it.

special-04-46
00:02:26.542 --> 00:02:30.263
Those who admit dependence are called
“not independent enough.”

special-04-47
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.452
But the critique must cut both ways.

special-04-48
00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:37.311
“Western individualism cannot resolve
relationships.”

special-04-49
00:02:37.311 --> 00:02:41.032
That claim is also being used to send
women home.

special-04-50
00:02:41.032 --> 00:02:45.181
Exposing the fantasy of independence does
not mean returning to assigned

special-04-51
00:02:45.181 --> 00:02:45.815
dependency.

special-04-52
00:02:46.878 --> 00:02:52.161
Nancy Fraser offered a direction: rather
than making women live as men do now, let

special-04-53
00:02:52.161 --> 00:02:55.383
men live as women do now, combining work
and care.

special-04-54
00:02:55.383 --> 00:03:00.167
Treat dependence as a fact of everyone's
life, not one gender's calling.

special-04-55
00:03:01.762 --> 00:03:04.951
So the real question is not whether women
should be independent.

special-04-56
00:03:04.951 --> 00:03:09.469
It is: who pays for someone's
“independence”?

special-04-57
00:03:10.266 --> 00:03:13.987
In the next main episode, we keep asking:

special-04-58
00:03:13.987 --> 00:03:17.176
Between “she can give birth” and “so she
should shoulder all of this,”

special-04-59
00:03:17.176 --> 00:03:21.428
who left out the argument on our behalf?

special-04-60
00:03:25.149 --> 00:03:29.390
This is UjiBricks.
